First training run hit 100 percent accuracy by epoch 2 which was severe overfitting so I stopped it and added geometric augmentations.
The second 15 epoch run worked perfectly giving 83.93 percent train and 80.97 percent val accuracy, saving the best checkpoint at epoch 9.
Moving to Kaggle evaluation was a nightmare with three cascading bugs. First, accidental ImageNet normalization killed the ReLU activations dropping accuracy to 40 percent. 
Second, the label mapping was hardcoded wrong giving 33 percent. Third, string versus integer label types caused a crash at 25 percent.
I wrote a universal handler to fix all of them.
Then I ran data forensics to check for leakage. The hashing script crashed on HuggingFace raw lists so I wrote a custom robust_to_pil converter. 
It revealed that 220 exact duplicate images, which is 55 percent of the test set, were leaked directly from the training set. 
When I filtered them out to test on the 174 clean images, accuracy collapsed to 13.79 percent and it only predicted meningioma. 
This happened because of a mix of loading old weights from memory, a wrong filename, and an architecture mismatch in the script.
After reviewing the math, a 4 class random guess is 25 percent so getting 13.79 percent means the model is confidently wrong. 
Since the model gets 84 percent on training and 84 percent on the BRISC dataset, the architecture is fine. 
The only logical conclusion is that the dataset creator accidentally flipped or mislabeled the test images, so the model is being penalized for predicting the correct labels it learned.
To wrap up, I overhauled the MLOps by making a single data.py file, removing hardcoded API tokens, adding strict CSV logging, and updating the documentation to reflect that the low test score is a data artifact, not a model failure.
